Canonical modern master and standalone distribution
---------------------------------------------------
The canonical modern master is the gem-lab distribution in Ablation:
https://github.com/Ablation-Tool/ablation

This standalone repository contains that master's actual native workbench,
including its GTK and Tk interfaces, METRIX terminal components, charts,
Spatial IR, VDI support, preferences, session protocol, installers, and stress
suites. It is not an independently redesigned interface. The standalone
packaging, version metadata, documentation, and supplied PNG/ICO/ICNS window
and desktop-installer icons are retained, and optional local-model prediction
and advisory capabilities are added.

Accessible-volume research, the equation, experiments, and paper are solely the
work of Nicholas Michael Kloster. The equation was created on March 6, 2026.
Canonical research and measurement harness:
https://github.com/Ablation-Tool/next-token-predictability

The prediction integration adapts that harness for live Lab selections. The
advisory and equation-tournament layers are experimental deterministic
heuristics and sensitivity instruments, not correctness, safety, authorization,
or autonomous-execution systems.

The original Ablation project is distributed under GPL-3.0-or-later. This
standalone distribution retains that license; see LICENSE.

Upstream copyright and component notices for Ubuntu Update Manager, GNOME
Baobab, Vanilla Framework icons, terminal palettes, and related materials are
preserved in UPSTREAM-NOTICE and licenses/.

The terminal emulator uses pyte (MIT) and tkinterdnd2 (MIT), plus pywinpty
(MIT) on native Windows. Those packages retain their own licenses.

The supplied application icon is stored as gem/assets/gem2red.png with native
ICO and ICNS variants. The original /media path is not a runtime dependency.

Landmark example attribution
----------------------------
The landmark-alignment worked example uses the caption "Aligning two shapes by
minimizing the distance of corresponding landmark points" from "Linear Algebra?"
by Daniel Cohen-Or, Olga Sorkine-Hornung, and Chen Greif. Approximate landmark
coordinates were extracted from a user-supplied screenshot for mathematical
analysis. The original figure and screenshot are not redistributed in this
repository.
